import{_ as i,P as n,d as a,e as o,y as r,q as s,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.10 HV10 Evolutionary Phase (Full Interface Card)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.10 HV10 Evolutionary Phase (Full Interface Card)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1768,"xkLessons":{"A.10":{"title":"A.10 HV10 Evolutionary phase (complete interface card)","lead":"First check applicability to human collectives, then assess components, prototypes, mechanisms, and exit through six distinct routes; the numbers are neither an obligatory sequence nor a ranking of value.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4248–4313; original-site location /theory/appendix-a/a-10#section-a-10. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; the historical Chinese research manuscript and slices remain downloadable in their original versions for checking.","Short quotation: ‘X0 is not an eighth stage’ (translated from Chinese). HV10 applies only to human intentional collectives that are forming or operating. Direction, generation, uptake, and institutionalization are dimensions to examine, not requirements that everything be mature. The numbering cannot be used for individual personalities, purely technical systems, or rankings of species' value; nor does the absence of mature uptake exclude all collectives in formation. [T-main]","HV10-R0-component-applicability requires auditable assessment records for HV03/HV04/HV05/HV07, allowing missing, not_applicable, and unsupported. All four components need not be empirically established, but these statuses must be distinguished. R0 can register applicability, missing components, mixed observations, and needs for further work; component assessments alone cannot complete an S prototype or X0 path. [T-main]","HV10-R1-pattern-phase-match checks the object's K, window, applicability, and the selected prototype's entry conditions, observations, counterexamples, and falsification under §8.2–8.3. Once the specific cross-window criteria are satisfied, register single_dominant, mixed, or parallel; crucial gaps require unknown. It does not impose a prior requirement to obtain two operational intervals or CM-PHASE. Conversely, prototype matching does not automatically establish a phase-threshold pattern. [T-main；T-state]","HV10-R2-causal-transition requires CM-PHASE pattern matching plus G2 preselected state effects through a specified transition channel; R3-path-dependent-phase requires CM-PHASE plus G3 historical conditional increments beyond the current representation; R5-carried-phase-persistence requires CM-PHASE, G2, and the current carriers, cross-window persistence or delayed recovery, and discriminating evidence specified in §4.5. All three routes additionally require CAUSAL/E4. Curve shapes, reversals, and persistent correlations do not directly establish them, and their conditions cannot be borrowed from one another. [T-main；T-phase；T-history]","HV10-R4-orderly-exit-path independently checks X0 entry and completion conditions under §8.5, recording the original K, functions, personnel, relationships, resources, memory, debts, appeals, and continuity. It does not require a minimum S prototype or CM-PHASE; candidate, in progress, partially complete, complete, and unknown must remain distinct. If any affected group, residual obligation, receiving capacity, appeal, or memory safety is unknown, benign completion cannot be recorded. [T-main；T-exit]","One collective may simultaneously exhibit S2 uptake taking shape and S5 accumulation of vulnerabilities; jumps, reversals, splits, mergers, dormancy, and functional transfers are also allowed. mixed and parallel require positive evidence for the corresponding prototypes and positions; lack of material cannot be renamed mixed. If K is redefined, version the change and check identity; until that check is complete, do not connect two sets of object records into a historical path. [T-main；T-state]","The nine axes preserve local phase distributions and differences of scale. An overall label does not represent every subgroup, and an expanded scope of effects does not expand J. Phase is not health, success, or normative justification. Even a reliable judgment does not authorize probing, advancement, merger, exit, or elimination: each still requires N/C12/J/O, rather than authority conferred by a high number. [T-main]","Members may safely challenge phase evidence, linear assumptions, and moralizing uses; independent review must be outside the original decision chain. Authorized rollback revokes labels and downstream decision effects, restores variable-level descriptions and unresolved status, and retains versions. Simply renaming something cannot restore a situation already harmed. [T-main]","The UK GOV.UK guidance on retiring a service separately lists notifying users, continuing needs, new services, and data handling. It helps reveal continuity responsibilities beyond closing a webpage. No material from an actual service retirement is provided here, so it cannot support a claim that an institution has met all X0 completion conditions. [P-RETIRE]","Field-by-field study of all 39 fields (“Original card” below retains the field’s complete content in translation; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Interface ID (id). Original card: HV10. Interpretation: This is a short identifier within the card collection, used to track the same interface; it does not establish a real-world object. [T-main]","02 | Qualified ID (qualified_id). Original card: human_variable:HV10. Interpretation: The qualified namespace prevents naming collisions; references to this ID must still carry the object and evidentiary status. [T-main]","03 | Name (name). Original card: Evolutionary phase. Interpretation: The name offers a reading entry point, not an automatic label for specific people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Claim type (claim_type). Original card: H. Interpretation: H identifies the role of a human-instance/interface proposition; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Contract role (contract_role). Original card: human_variable_interface. Interpretation: this variable-interface contract specifies how to enter analysis; a field’s existence does not mean an empirical instance has been established. [T-main]","06 | Proposition (proposition). Original card: S0-S6 and X0 apply only to human intentional collectives that are forming or operating; direction, generation, uptake, and institutionalization are dimensions to examine, without requiring all to be mature; jumps between stages, parallelism, mixtures, reversals, splits, mergers, dormancy, absorption, and functional transfers are allowed. Interpretation: This proposition must be read together with the conditional routes and ceilings below; an unconditional causal or normative conclusion cannot be extracted from one sentence. [T-main]","07 | Scope (scope). Original card: Human intentional collectives meeting the applicability conditions. Interpretation: Scope lists task domains that may be examined; it does not mean every object within that scope has empirical support. [T-main]","08 | Pause condition (pause_condition). Original card: When stages are moralized or titles conflict with criteria, pause the corresponding prototype assertions; when applicability conditions are missing, do not make prototype or path conclusions that depend on those conditions, while inapplicability, missing components, and observation needs may still be registered under the complete R0 conditions. Interpretation: Pausing targets the dependent judgment or adoption. Independently established descriptions may remain, but invalid premises cannot continue to be used. [T-main]","09 | Inferential dependencies (inferential_requires). Original card: 1. human_variable:HV03; 2. human_variable:HV04; 3. human_variable:HV05; 4. human_variable:HV07. Interpretation: HV03/HV04/HV05/HV07 require auditable component records; do not misstate this as requiring all four empirical propositions to hold. [T-main]","10 | Protocol dependencies (protocol_requires). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. EVIDENCE; 2. SOURCE. Interpretation: Protocol dependencies constrain evidence collection and expression; completing protocols does not independently establish the truth of empirical propositions. [T-main]","11 | Qualification / specialization (specializes). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: an empty set means only that no such specialization relations are registered, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a nonempty set also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Applicable-object references (applies_to). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: applicable-object references cannot replace the current object contract; an empty set is explicit registration, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Conditional support routes (conditional_support_routes). Original card: 1. route_id=HV10-R0-component-applicability;; claim_level=descriptive_classification;; when=HV03, HV04, HV05, and HV07 have auditable assessment records; records may be missing, not_applicable, or unsupported, and empirical establishment of all four components is not required.;; additional_inferential_requires=none (empty set);; additional_protocol_requires=none (empty set);; allowed_conclusion=Register applicability, inapplicability, missing components, mixed states, and needs for continued observation.;; result_ceiling=Component checking alone produces neither an S0-S6 prototype match nor a completed X0 exit-path judgment.; 2. route_id=HV10-R1-pattern-phase-match;; claim_level=descriptive_classification;; when=Complete the checks of applicability, object K, observation window, and the selected prototype’s necessary entry conditions, observations, counterexamples, and falsification conditions under §8.2–8.3; specific criteria needing evidence across windows must meet their requirements, but a second operating interval or a CM-PHASE pattern is not required in advance.;; additional_inferential_requires=none (empty set);; additional_protocol_requires=E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register single, mixed, or parallel prototype configurations of S0-S6; record other descriptive paths separately under their respective conditions in §8.4, and judge X0 as an independent exit path.;; result_ceiling=Limited to description of prototype configurations; does not automatically confer eligibility for a CM-PHASE pattern, causal phase transition, or historical increment. Prototypes are not rankings of health, success, legitimacy, or elimination.; 3. route_id=HV10-R2-causal-transition;; claim_level=mechanism_explanation;; when=CM-PHASE matching holds, and a G2-instance identifies the effect of a specified phase-transition channel on a preselected state change.;; additional_inferential_requires=CM-PHASE, G2-instance;; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate causal phase transition within the specified object, window, and channel.;; result_ceiling=Do not infer a necessary stage sequence, value direction, or authorization for advancement or elimination from a transition mechanism.; 4. route_id=HV10-R3-path-dependent-phase;; claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;; when=CM-PHASE matching holds, and a G3-instance shows a conditional increment from historical phase variables for subsequent states, hysteresis, or regression.;; additional_inferential_requires=CM-PHASE, G3-instance;; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register candidates for historical-increment path dependence, hysteresis, or historical phase differences beyond the current state within a preregistered window.;; result_ceiling=Do not call them fate or absolute irreversibility, or automatically prescribe repair, exit, or retirement plans.; 5. route_id=HV10-R4-orderly-exit-path; claim_level=descriptive_classification; when=Check X0 candidate-entry and completion conditions individually under §8.5, separately recording the original object K, functions, personnel, relationships, resources, memory, debts, appeals, and continuity states. No minimum S0-S6 prototype or CM-PHASE match is required first.; additional_inferential_requires=none (empty set); additional_protocol_requires=E4; allowed_conclusion=Register an independent lifecycle-path judgment of X0 as candidate, in progress, partially complete, complete, or unknown.; result_ceiling=If affected groups, legacy obligations, receiving capacity, appeals, or memory safety remain unknown, do not judge completion as benign. X0 is not an eighth stage and generates no authorization to dissolve, transfer, publish archives, or abandon responsibility.; 6. route_id=HV10-R5-carried-phase-persistence;; claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;; when=CM-PHASE pattern conditions hold; evidence of the current carrier, persistence across windows or delayed recovery, and discrimination required for CM-PHASE-carried-persistence is complete; and a qualified G2-instance covers the specified outcome and window.;; additional_inferential_requires=CM-PHASE, G2-instance;; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate mechanism of phase persistence or delayed recovery maintained through a current carrier within the same K and specified window.;; result_ceiling=Do not directly treat phase persistence, regression, or curve shape as a mechanism; do not register a G3 historical increment; and do not generate necessary stages, value directions, or real-world action authorization. Interpretation: The six routes—R0 component assessment, R1 prototype description, R2 causal transition, R3 historical increment, R4 independent X0, and R5 current-carrier persistence—each retain their own complete conditions. [T-main]","14 | Allowed inference (allowed_inference). Original card: 1. Conditional state coordinates; 2. Nonlinear paths; 3. Orderly exit X0. Interpretation: These indicate permissible directions of conclusions; the selected route and material still limit which one is reached and its strength. [T-main]","15 | Prohibited leap (prohibited_leap). Original card: 1. Every system must pass through S0-S6; 2. A higher stage is more legitimate; 3. Dissolution equals failure. Interpretation: These are common overreaches, not problems avoided by rewording. Examine actual inferences and subsequent uses, not vocabulary alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale profile (scale_profile). Original card: SP=<A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J>; A=aggregation level: single state events, local group cases, intentional collective populations, phase distributions, and aggregation rules; X=spatial scope: action sites, organizational or institutional boundaries, digital collaboration spaces, and cross-domain evolutionary scope; T=time span: state windows, transition lags, regression, dormancy, hysteresis, and intergenerational cycles; O=organizational level: member roles, teams, organizations, institutions, through governance ecosystems; C=causal level: state events, transition interaction mechanisms, meso-level phase structures, institutionalization processes, and system conditions; R=observational resolution: original state events, transition sequences, local cases, phase distributions, state indicators, and summaries, with compression losses registered; I=impact scope: direct members and uptake providers, those exiting, indirectly affected people, second-order consequences, cross-domain and intergenerational effects; N=network-topological scope: local phase clusters, cross-level connections, splitting and merging, functional transfers, and inheritance paths; J=jurisdiction and authorization scope: register authorization separately for phase naming, adoption of monitoring, probing, advancement, exit, and elimination; retain mixed phases. Interpretation: Register all nine axes individually. In particular, impact scope I does not generate jurisdiction J, and compression losses must remain recorded when observational resolution R decreases. [T-main]","17 | Effective object (effective_object). Original card: Human intentional collective states in formation or operation; components may be present, forming, missing, or inapplicable, judged according to the prototype’s specific conditions. Interpretation: This describes the object of the interface. When selecting a low-threshold descriptive route, functional meanings in names must not be treated as established causation. [T-main]","18 | Cross-scale invariants (scale_invariants). Original card: 1. Applicability conditions; 2. Phase criteria; 3. Nonlinear paths; 4. X0 is not counted as an eighth stage. Interpretation: Invariants are comparative responsibilities to preserve during transfer, not a claim that variable values remain unchanged at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Required additions for elevation (required_scale_additions). Original card: 1. Local phase distributions; 2. Cross-level transitions; 3. Uptake inheritance; 4. Protection and exit. Interpretation: Elevation is not copying the original table at a larger size. When added material about populations, interfaces, and responsibility positions is incomplete, limit conclusions depending on it. [T-main]","20 | Semantics changing with scale (changing_semantics). Original card: 1. Uptake providers, institutional carriers, and effective objects may change with phase. Interpretation: The same term may change meaning when the object or scale changes. Reestablish the correspondence rather than assuming the old meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Non-applicable objects (non_applicable_objects). Original card: 1. Populations, purely technical systems, or similar categories without grounds for a human intentional collective; a lack of mature uptake during formation does not by itself justify blanket exclusion. Interpretation: Human prototypes cannot be directly attached to purely technical systems; a forming collective is not categorically excluded merely because mature uptake is missing. [T-main]","22 | Forbidden elevation (forbidden_elevation). Original card: 1. A case’s phase directly represents the population; 2. Human stages are transferred into the universal core. Interpretation: Inferences from a part to the whole need separate evidence. Broad influence, grand names, or formal resemblance cannot supply representativeness or authorization. [T-main]","23 | State set (state). Original card: Prototypes: S0, S1, S2, S3, S4, S5, S6; classification modes: single_dominant, mixed, parallel, unknown; independent path: X0, registered through R4, not listed as an eighth state. Interpretation: There are seven prototypes and four classification modes; X0 is an independent path, not an eighth state. [T-main]","24 | Observables (observables). Original card: 1. Contemporaneous states of direction, generation, uptake, institutionalization, and feedback variables; 2. Records of repeated phase-criterion matches across observation windows; 3. Trajectories of stage skipping, parallelism, mixture, regression, splitting, merging, and dormancy; 4. Records of functional transfer, responsibility inheritance, and orderly exit in X0. Interpretation: Observable clues indicate where to seek material. One clue’s presence is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements (evidence). Original card: 1. Phase variables; 2. Transition records; 3. Traces; 4. Uptake and institutional states. Interpretation: Obtain the listed evidence according to route and task relevance. Keep unknown, not collected, not applicable, and unavailable for protective reasons separate; do not force disclosure to fill the table. [T-main]","26 | Input dependencies and interface contents (input_dependencies). Original card: 1. Orientation anchors; 2. Generative nodes; 3. Uptake layers; 4. Feedback written back into revision; 5. Structural load; 6. Pattern phases do not require G3; causal transitions separately require CAUSAL. R5 checks persistence or delayed recovery transmitted through current carriers under §4.5; only R3 additionally requires G3 to test an extra historical conditional increment. H5 supports only the corresponding candidate traces. Interpretation: This specifies upstream inputs and their status; gaps must not quietly disappear as references pass between interfaces. [T-main]","27 | Output effects and variable flows (output_effects). Original card: 1. Phase judgments 2. Uptake inheritance 3. Exit and repair needs. Interpretation: Variable flows indicate which downstream judgments may depend on this interface. Passing a reference does not guarantee a downstream conclusion, actual repair, or authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag (time_window_and_lag). Original card: Register phase observation windows, transition lags, regression, and dormancy. Interpretation: Occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may happen at different times. Short-term material cannot automatically certify what lies beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty (uncertainty). Original card: Record mixed phases, splitting, merging, and scale differences. Interpretation: Tie uncertainty to specific material and positions; do not write only an overall confidence score that hides positions never covered. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone (local_exclusion_zone). Original card: Local groups and roles not represented by the overall phase. Interpretation: An exclusion zone limits the coverage of current inference; it does not delete these people or facts from the world. Unavailability for safety reasons is neither consent nor absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions (affected_positions). Original card: 1. Members; 2. Uptake providers; 3. Dissenters; 4. Those exiting; 5. Successors. Interpretation: Register both directly and indirectly affected people. Visible members do not represent everyone bearing costs. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carrier (carrier). Original card: 1. Collective action; 2. Organizational structures; 3. Institutional records; 4. Shared memory. Interpretation: A carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something. Its name, completion of construction, and delivery of the required function are different evidentiary matters. [T-main]","33 | Responsible party (responsible_subject). Original card: 1. Analysts making phase judgments; 2. Decision-makers and authorizers acting on them. Interpretation: State who proposes the judgment and who operates or uses it. Registering responsibility does not automatically adjudicate legal liability or create new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status (normative_status). Original card: Phases do not constitute rankings of health, success, or legitimacy. Interpretation: Distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status according to the card. An established fact does not independently determine value rankings; normative reasons do not automatically prove empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling (judgment_ceiling). Original card: Limit each route by its own complete conditions: R0 only classifies component applicability and absence; R1 matches S0-S6 prototypes under §8.2–8.3 without a rigid CM-PHASE requirement; R2 offers a bounded candidate causal phase-transition explanation under CM-PHASE and G2-instance conditions; R3 explains historical conditional increments under CM-PHASE and G3-instance conditions; R4 independently judges X0 path states under §8.5 without presupposing an S0-S6 or CM-PHASE match. R5 judges a mechanism maintained across windows through current carriers using CM-PHASE, G2-instance, and §4.5 evidence. Routes do not supply one another’s missing conditions, and none generates real-world action authorization. Interpretation: R1 does not rigidly require CM-PHASE; R4 does not require a minimum S prototype; R2/R3/R5 follow their own mechanism conditions, with no borrowing of gate satisfaction between routes. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling (action_ceiling). Original card: Depending on the selected route, this variable generates component applicability, prototype matches, mixed states, bounded explanations of phase mechanisms or historical conditional increments, independent X0 path states, uncertainty, and observation needs. It does not authorize probing, advancement, merging, exit, or elimination. Every real adjustment must separately pass C12, explicit runtime N premises, J authorization, and O procedures. Interpretation: The interface itself generates no power to act in reality. Actual actions require separate checks of choice, C12, explicit N, valid J, O, protection, and duration. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples (counterexamples). Original card: 1. The same collective simultaneously exhibits a mixed phase of S2 uptake formation and S5 vulnerability accumulation; 2. Orderly exit X0 preserves functional transfer rather than constituting stage failure. Interpretation: Use counterexamples to limit unconditional extrapolation. They may require narrower scope or renewed evidence collection, not automatic rejection of every limited use. [T-main]","38 | Appeal (appeal). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, members may challenge phase evidence, linear assumptions, and moralizing uses through safe, accessible channels protected against retaliation, triggering review independent of the original phase-judgment or decision chain. Interpretation: Appeals must be safely accessible, free from retaliation, and able to trigger independent review. A name alone does not establish authority to change errors. [T-main]","39 | Rollback (rollback). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, when rollback is authorized, designated responsible persons must revoke phase naming and its downstream decision effects within specified time limits, actually restore variable-level descriptions and unresolved states, and preserve versions and completion verification. Interpretation: Within valid authority, actually revoke erroneous effects, correct downstream consequences, and verify completion. Editing document text does not mean rights and resources have returned to the person concerned. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Assess the four components auditably, distinguishing missing, not applicable, and unsupported.","Freeze K, the nine axes, subsystems, and window; R1 checks prototype entry conditions, counterexamples, and falsification conditions individually.","R2/R3/R5 separately check CM-PHASE and evidence for channels, historical increments, or current carriers.","R4 independently checks X0 entry and seven completion dimensions, without setting a minimum stage.","Retain positive evidence for the positions in mixed/parallel configurations; missing evidence means unknown, not a guessed label.","Classification is a revocable description only. Interventions require separate authorization, and erroneous downstream effects must also be corrected."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Prototype match","leftText":"An S-configuration description for a specified K and window.","rightTitle":"Phase pattern","rightText":"CM-PHASE’s contract of two intervals and preset thresholds."},{"leftTitle":"Mixed or parallel","leftText":"Different prototypes and their corresponding positions have positive support.","rightTitle":"Unknown","rightText":"Key material is insufficient to distinguish them; this cannot be renamed mixed."},{"leftTitle":"X0 completion","leftText":"Functions, personnel, resources, memory, responsibility, and other dimensions are checked against their conditions.","rightTitle":"Activities stop","rightText":"May indicate only closure or functional transfer, not completed benign exit."},{"leftTitle":"Current-carrier persistence","leftText":"The bounded mechanism of R5.","rightTitle":"Historical conditional increment","rightText":"R3 separately checks G3; it is not inferred from a persistence curve."}],"boundaries":["Counterexample in the original card: the same collective simultaneously exhibits S2 and S5 and cannot be compressed into one maturity ranking.","Counterexample in the original card: X0 may preserve functional transfer and need not be treated as stage failure.","Teaching counterexample: all four components have records, but all their evidence is unknown, and S6 is nevertheless declared.","Boundary in the source: when receiving capacity or memory safety is unknown, X0 must not be judged benignly complete."],"questions":["Does R0 require all four components to be proven?","Must R1 have two operating intervals?","Can exit occur from S0?","Is a higher phase more legitimate?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-10/figures/hv10-routes.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Continuity checks in GOV.UK service-retirement design","href":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/agile-delivery/retiring-your-service"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Continuity checks in GOV.UK service-retirement design","text":"The guidance requires notifying users, addressing continuing needs, and managing data, indicating responsibilities beyond service shutdown.","sourceLabel":"GOV.UK Service Manual: Retiring your service","sourceUrl":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/agile-delivery/retiring-your-service","sourceLocator":"Body sections including “Consider user needs,” “Telling your users,” “Make a plan to redirect traffic,” and “Protecting information,” webpage lines 72–165","boundary":"The guidance is not an empirical retirement case, nor has it verified every X0 requirement under this framework.","href":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/agile-delivery/retiring-your-service"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.10","start_line":4341,"end_line":4406,"startLine":4341,"endLine":4406}],"outputId":"section-a-10","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: do not hide unknowns behind mixed","text":"An artificial team has only a roster of uptake positions; feedback, entry-condition, and counterevidence material has not been collected. Corresponding component observations may be registered; inability to identify the dominant prototype does not justify writing mixed. Only for another artificial object where S2 and S5 each meet their conditions and have evidence for their corresponding scopes can a mixed configuration be discussed.","assumptions":"Entirely fictional, to distinguish positive evidence for multiple prototypes from missing information; it certifies no real instance.","boundary":"Prototype conditions must be checked individually; a roster alone cannot determine S2."},{"title":"Teaching construction: exit is not website closure","text":"An artificial project shuts down its website and has a recipient for its functions, but who takes over appeals and whether record access is safe remain unknown. Functional transfer and an X0 candidacy or partial progress may be recorded according to the material, but not benign completion. Closure must not cancel legacy obligations.","assumptions":"A fictional project; the specific X0 state still depends on entry and other completion conditions.","boundary":"No actual deregistration, archive publication, or resource transfer is recommended; these require independent authority and protection."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the original card: the same collective simultaneously exhibits S2 and S5 and cannot be compressed into one maturity ranking.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the original card: X0 may preserve functional transfer and need not be treated as stage failure.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: all four components have records, but all their evidence is unknown, and S6 is nevertheless declared.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: when receiving capacity or memory safety is unknown, X0 must not be judged benignly complete.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does R0 require all four components to be proven?","answer":"Full empirical establishment is not required, but auditable assessment records are; missing/not_applicable/unsupported are allowed.","basis":"R0。"},{"question":"Must R1 have two operating intervals?","answer":"Not rigidly. It follows each prototype’s conditions; CM-PHASE is a separate contract.","basis":"R1 and 6.7."},{"question":"Can exit occur from S0?","answer":"X0 has no minimum S state, but still requires its own entry and completion conditions.","basis":"R4。"},{"question":"Is a higher phase more legitimate?","answer":"No. Numbering, capability, norms, and action authority must remain separate.","basis":"Prohibited leap."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-10/figures/hv10-routes.png","alt":"Six routes: R0 components, R1 prototypes, R2 transitions, R3 history, R4 exit, and R5 carrier persistence.","caption":"Based on the six routes in A.10, not linear stages."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-10/figures/hv10-state.png","alt":"Prototypes, phase patterns, causal explanations, and normative authorization each have different contracts.","caption":"Based on A.10 and 6.7/8.2."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-10/figures/hv10-exit.png","alt":"X0 has no minimum-prototype requirement. Completion checks functions, personnel, resources, memory, debts, and appeals; unknown does not mean complete.","caption":"Based on A.10 R4 and 8.5."}],"diagramBrief":"The three diagrams distinguish routes, prototypes from patterns, and X0’s independent completion dimensions; they do not draw a one-way evolutionary ladder from low to high.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["There are no actual prototype runtime records or CM-PHASE/G2/G3 certifications.","Retirement design guidance cannot replace actual material on specific affected groups and receiving capacity."],"taskId":"S-A-10","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.10, Chinese historical source lines 4162–4227","claim_support":["All 39 fields and six routes of HV10."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-state","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§8.2–8.3, Chinese historical source lines 1637–1786","claim_support":["Prototype object contracts, missing states, mixed evidence, and individual conditions."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-phase","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§6.7, Chinese historical source lines 1384–1397","claim_support":["Phase patterns, triggers, and two persistence routes."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-exit","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§8.5–8.5.2, Chinese historical source lines 1811–1838","claim_support":["X0’s independent entry, seven completion dimensions, and unknown boundaries."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-history","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.5, Chinese source lines 620–641","claim_support":["The distinction between carrier persistence and historical conditional increments."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-RETIRE","title":"GOV.UK Service Manual: Retiring your service","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/agile-delivery/retiring-your-service","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Published in 2016; updated 2025-08-26","locator":"Body sections including “Consider user needs,” “Telling your users,” “Make a plan to redirect traffic,” and “Protecting information,” webpage lines 72–165","claim_support":["The service-retirement guidance requires notifying users, addressing continuing needs and alternative support, redirecting to new services, and handling data lawfully."],"limits":["The original guide’s author read the body of the guidance. It is design guidance, not proof that any actual service has completed retirement, nor does it automatically constitute X0 eligibility under this theory."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"A.10 HV10 Evolutionary Phase (Complete Interface Card); Chinese source lines 4248–4313; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-10/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-10/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-10/source/S-A-10.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-10/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"e80b6b486fa49cbedc938169dbe315334997d2b7c1ba833a394d899fed4fea60","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-10.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-10.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-10.md"};function l(p,e,u,h,m,g){const t=n("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=r('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-10" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.10</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV10 Evolutionary Phase (Full Interface Card) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-10" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.10 HV10 Evolutionary Phase (Full Interface Card) {#section-a-10}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">First check applicability to human collectives, then assess components, prototypes, mechanisms, and exit through six distinct routes; the numbers are neither an obligatory sequence nor a ranking of value.</span><details class="xk-gist__codes"><summary>Codes used in this section (18)</summary><ul><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-10"><b>HV10</b>Evolutionary Phase</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-08.html#section-8-3-1"><b>S0</b>Prototype of Coalescence Around an Anchor</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-08.html#section-8-3-7"><b>S6</b>Prototype of Adaptive Dynamic Balance</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-08.html#section-8-5"><b>X0</b>Orderly Exit and Benign Demise</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-3"><b>HV03</b>Orientation Anchor</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-4"><b>HV04</b>Generative Node</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-5"><b>HV05</b>Action Uptake Layer</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-3-7"><b>HV07</b>Feedback Write-Back</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-06.html#section-6-7"><b>CM-PHASE</b>Phase and Threshold Transition</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#section-4-8-4"><b>E4</b>Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#section-4-4"><b>G2</b>Specified-Channel Effects and Per-Dimension Boundedness</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#section-4-5"><b>G3</b>Historical Formation, Current Carriers, and Conditional Increments</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-08.html#section-8-3-2"><b>S1</b>Prototype of a Generative Core Taking Shape</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-08.html#section-8-3-3"><b>S2</b>Prototype of Initial Expansion and Differentiated Uptake</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-08.html#section-8-3-4"><b>S3</b>Prototype of Development Under Interconnected Constraints</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-08.html#section-8-3-5"><b>S4</b>Prototype of a Window for Structural Reconfiguration</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-08.html#section-8-3-6"><b>S5</b>Prototype of Efficient Coordination and Flexible Expansion</a></li><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#section-4-14-1"><b>C12</b>Check on the Validity of Normative Bridging</a></li></ul></details></div><p>A. Identity, Proposition, and Scope</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interface ID (<code>id</code>)</td><td>HV10</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified ID (<code>qualified_id</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable:HV10</code></td></tr><tr><td>Name (<code>name</code>)</td><td>Evolutionary Phase</td></tr><tr><td>Claim type (<code>claim_type</code>)</td><td>H</td></tr><tr><td>Contract role (<code>contract_role</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable_interface</code></td></tr><tr><td>Proposition (<code>proposition</code>)</td><td>S0–S6 and X0 apply only to human intentional collectives that are taking shape or operating. Orientation, generation, uptake, and institutionalization are aspects to be checked. They need not all be mature. Skipping stages, parallelism, mixing, regression, splits, mergers, dormancy, absorption, and functional transfer are possible.</td></tr><tr><td>Scope (<code>scope</code>)</td><td>Human intentional collectives that meet the conditions of applicability</td></tr><tr><td>Conditions for suspension (<code>pause_condition</code>)</td><td>Suspend the relevant prototype assertion when a stage is given a moral ranking or its title conflicts with the criteria. If an applicability condition is missing, do not draw conclusions about prototypes or paths that depend on it. When all conditions of R0 are met, inapplicability, missing components, and observation needs may still be registered.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>B. Formal Dependencies and Limits on Conclusions</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inferential dependencies (<code>inferential_requires</code>)</td><td>1. <code>human_variable:HV03</code><br>2. <code>human_variable:HV04</code><br>3. <code>human_variable:HV05</code><br>4. <code>human_variable:HV07</code></td></tr><tr><td>Protocol dependencies (<code>protocol_requires</code>)</td><td>1. EVIDENCE<br>2. SOURCE</td></tr><tr><td>Qualification/specialization (<code>specializes</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>References to applicable objects (<code>applies_to</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional support routes (<code>conditional_support_routes</code>)</td><td>1. route_id=HV10-R0-component-applicability;<br>claim_level=descriptive_classification;<br>when=Auditable assessment records exist for HV03, HV04, HV05, and HV07. The records may carry the statuses missing, not_applicable, or unsupported. All four components need not be established empirically;<br>additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);<br>additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);<br>allowed_conclusion=Register applicability, inapplicability, missing components, mixed states, and needs for further observation;<br>result_ceiling=Component checks alone do not establish a match to an S0–S6 prototype or complete a judgment about the X0 exit path.<br>2. route_id=HV10-R1-pattern-phase-match;<br>claim_level=descriptive_classification;<br>when=Applicability, the object’s K, the observation window, and the necessary checks for entry, observations, counterexamples, and falsification for the selected prototype are completed under §8.2–8.3. A criterion that needs evidence across windows must meet its own requirements. A second operating interval or a CM-PHASE pattern need not be established first;<br>additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);<br>additional_protocol_requires=E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a single, mixed, or parallel configuration of S0–S6 prototypes. Register other descriptive paths separately under their own conditions in §8.4, and judge X0 as an independent exit path;<br>result_ceiling=Description of prototype configuration only. This does not automatically qualify as a CM-PHASE pattern, a causal phase transition, or a historical increment. Prototypes do not rank health, success, normative justification, or suitability for elimination.<br>3. route_id=HV10-R2-causal-transition;<br>claim_level=mechanism_explanation;<br>when=A CM-PHASE match is established, and a G2-instance identifies the effect of the specified phase-transition channel on the preselected state change;<br>additional_inferential_requires=CM-PHASE, G2-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate causal phase transition within the specified object, window, and channel;<br>result_ceiling=Do not infer a necessary sequence of stages, a direction of value, or authorization to advance or eliminate anything from the transition mechanism.<br>4. route_id=HV10-R3-path-dependent-phase;<br>claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;<br>when=A CM-PHASE match is established, and a G3-instance shows that historical phase variables provide a conditional increment for subsequent states, hysteresis, or regression;<br>additional_inferential_requires=CM-PHASE, G3-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register candidates for path dependence, hysteresis, or historical phase differences with a historical increment beyond the current state within the pre-registered window;<br>result_ceiling=Do not call this fate or absolute irreversibility, or use it to prescribe options for repair, exit, or wind-down automatically.<br>5. route_id=HV10-R4-orderly-exit-path;claim_level=descriptive_classification;when=Check the conditions for X0 candidate entry and for completion item by item under §8.5 and record separately the original object’s K, functions, personnel, relationships, resources, memory, debts, appeals, and continuity status. No S0–S6 prototype serves as a minimum prerequisite, and no prior CM-PHASE match is required;additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);additional_protocol_requires=E4;allowed_conclusion=Register a judgment of the independent life-cycle path X0 as candidate, in progress, partially complete, complete, or unknown;result_ceiling=Do not judge the outcome a benign completion while the affected groups, outstanding obligations, receiving capability, appeals, or safety of memory remain unknown. X0 is not an eighth stage and confers no authorization for dissolution, transfer, publication of archives, or relinquishment of responsibility.<br>6. route_id=HV10-R5-carried-phase-persistence;<br>claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;<br>when=The conditions for a CM-PHASE pattern are met, the current carrier, persistence across windows or delayed recovery, and discriminating evidence required by CM-PHASE-carried-persistence are all in place, and an eligible G2-instance covers the specified outcome and window;<br>additional_inferential_requires=CM-PHASE, G2-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate mechanism of phase persistence or delayed recovery maintained by a current carrier under the same K and within the specified window;<br>result_ceiling=Do not directly treat phase persistence, regression, or a curve shape as a mechanism. Do not register a G3 historical increment or infer a required sequence of stages, a direction of value, or authorization to act in reality.</td></tr><tr><td>Permitted inferences (<code>allowed_inference</code>)</td><td>1. Conditional state coordinates<br>2. Nonlinear paths<br>3. Orderly exit X0</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited leaps (<code>prohibited_leap</code>)</td><td>1. Assuming all systems must pass through S0–S6<br>2. Treating a higher stage as more normatively justified<br>3. Equating dissolution with failure</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>C. Nine-Axis Scale and Object Contract</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nine-axis scale profile (<code>scale_profile</code>)</td><td>SP=&lt;A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J&gt;. A=Aggregation level covers individual state events, cases of local groups, populations of intentional collectives, phase distributions, and aggregation rules. X=Spatial extent covers sites of action, organizational or institutional boundaries, digital spaces for collaboration, and the reach of evolution across domains. T=Time span covers state windows, transition lags, regression, dormancy, hysteresis, and generational cycles. O=Organizational level covers member roles, teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. C=Causal level covers state events, mechanisms of interaction in transitions, meso-level phase structures, institutionalization, and systemic conditions. R=Resolution covers original state events, transition sequences, local cases, phase distributions, state metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. I=Scope of influence covers direct members and people taking up work, people who exit, indirectly affected people, second-order consequences, and impacts across domains and generations. N=Network topology scope covers local phase clusters, connections across levels, splits and mergers, functional transfers, and succession paths. J=Scope of jurisdiction and authorization requires separate authorization records for naming phases, adopting monitoring, trials, advancement, orderly exit, and elimination. Preserve mixed phases</td></tr><tr><td>Effective object (<code>effective_object</code>)</td><td>States of human intentional collectives that are taking shape or operating. Components may be present, forming, missing, or inapplicable, as judged under the specific prototype conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Scale invariants (<code>scale_invariants</code>)</td><td>1. Conditions of applicability<br>2. Phase criteria<br>3. Nonlinear paths<br>4. X0 does not count as an eighth stage</td></tr><tr><td>Required additions for scale elevation (<code>required_scale_additions</code>)</td><td>1. Distribution of local phases<br>2. Transitions across levels<br>3. Continuity of uptake through succession<br>4. Protection and exit</td></tr><tr><td>What changes with scale (<code>changing_semantics</code>)</td><td>1. Those taking up work, institutional carriers, and effective objects can change with the phase</td></tr><tr><td>Inapplicable objects (<code>non_applicable_objects</code>)</td><td>1. Populations, purely technical systems, or categories based on similarity when there are no grounds for identifying a human intentional collective. Lack of mature uptake during formation is no basis for categorical exclusion</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited elevations (<code>forbidden_elevation</code>)</td><td>1. Treating the phase of one case as directly representative of the population<br>2. Transferring human stages into the universal core</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>D. State, Evidence, and Variable Flows</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State set (<code>state</code>)</td><td>Prototypes are S0, S1, S2, S3, S4, S5, and S6. Classification modes are <code>single_dominant</code>, <code>mixed</code>, <code>parallel</code>, and <code>unknown</code>. X0 is an independent path registered under R4 and is not listed as an eighth state</td></tr><tr><td>Observables (<code>observables</code>)</td><td>1. Concurrent states of orientation, generation, uptake, institutionalization, and feedback variables<br>2. Records of repeated matches to phase criteria across observation windows<br>3. Trajectories of skipped stages, parallelism, mixing, regression, splitting, merging, and dormancy<br>4. Records of functional transfer, carryover of responsibilities, and orderly exit in X0</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence requirements (<code>evidence</code>)</td><td>1. Phase variables<br>2. Transition records<br>3. Traces<br>4. Uptake and institutional status</td></tr><tr><td>Input dependencies and interface content (<code>input_dependencies</code>)</td><td>1. Orientation anchor<br>2. Generative node<br>3. Uptake layer<br>4. Feedback write-back<br>5. Structural load<br>6. Pattern-level phase classification does not require G3. A causal transition separately requires CAUSAL. R5 checks persistence or delayed recovery through a current carrier under §4.5. R3 alone additionally requires G3 to test the extra historical conditional increment. H5 supports only the corresponding candidate traces.</td></tr><tr><td>Output effects and variable flows (<code>output_effects</code>)</td><td>1. Phase judgments. 2. Continuity of uptake through succession. 3. Needs for orderly exit and repair</td></tr><tr><td>Time window and lag (<code>time_window_and_lag</code>)</td><td>Register phase observation windows, transition lags, regression, and dormancy</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty (<code>uncertainty</code>)</td><td>Record mixed phases, splits, mergers, and scale differences</td></tr><tr><td>Local exclusion zone (<code>local_exclusion_zone</code>)</td><td>Local groups and roles that an overall phase cannot represent</td></tr><tr><td>Affected positions (<code>affected_positions</code>)</td><td>1. Members<br>2. People taking up the work<br>3. Dissenters<br>4. People who exit<br>5. Successors</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>E. Uptake, Responsibility, Normative Status, Ceilings, and Correction</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Uptake carriers (<code>carrier</code>)</td><td>1. Collective action<br>2. Organizational structure<br>3. Institutional records<br>4. Shared memory</td></tr><tr><td>Responsible parties (<code>responsible_subject</code>)</td><td>1. Analysts who make the phase judgment<br>2. Decision makers and parties granting authorization for action based on it</td></tr><tr><td>Normative status (<code>normative_status</code>)</td><td>Phases do not rank health, success, or normative justification</td></tr><tr><td>Judgment ceiling (<code>judgment_ceiling</code>)</td><td>Apply each route’s complete conditions separately. R0 classifies only component applicability and missing components. R1 matches S0–S6 prototypes under §8.2–8.3 without requiring CM-PHASE. R2 gives a limited explanation of a candidate causal phase transition under CM-PHASE and G2-instance conditions. R3 explains historical conditional increments under CM-PHASE and G3-instance conditions. R4 independently judges X0 path status under §8.5 without requiring S0–S6 or a match to CM-PHASE. R5 judges mechanisms maintained by a current carrier across windows under CM-PHASE, G2-instance, and §4.5 evidence. Routes cannot supply one another’s missing conditions, and none authorizes action in reality.</td></tr><tr><td>Action ceiling (<code>action_ceiling</code>)</td><td>Depending on the selected route, this variable produces component applicability, prototype matches, mixed states, limited explanations of phase mechanisms or historical conditional increments, independent X0 path status, uncertainty, and observation needs. It does not authorize trials, advancement, merger, orderly exit, or elimination. Any real-world adjustment separately requires C12, explicit N premises at runtime, J authorization, and O procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Counterexamples (<code>counterexamples</code>)</td><td>1. The same collective simultaneously exhibits a mixed phase of uptake taking shape in S2 and vulnerabilities accumulating in S5<br>2. Orderly exit X0 preserves functional transfer and does not amount to failure of a stage</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal (<code>appeal</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, members can challenge phase evidence, linear assumptions, and uses that impose a moral ranking through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection. This triggers review independent of the original phase judgment or decision chain</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback (<code>rollback</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, once rollback is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline revoke the phase designation and its effects on downstream decisions and actually restore variable-level description and unresolved status. Preserve versions and verification of completion</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"A.10"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=i(d,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,y as default};
